<header><p class="st-kicker">kingdom · South Asia</p><h1 class="st-title">Videha</h1><p class="st-meta">1100 BCE – 600 BCE (approx.) · capital Mithila</p></header>
<p class="st-teaser">A Later Vedic kingdom in Mithila, north of the Ganges, famed for the philosopher-king Janaka and the debates that shaped the Upanishads.</p>
<div class="st-body"><div class="st-text"><h2>Origins</h2><p>The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/shatapatha-brahmana">Shatapatha Brahmana</a> tells how Videgha Mathava, guided by the fire god <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/karashahr">Agni</a>, carried Vedic culture east across the Sadanira river, probably the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gandaki-river">Gandak</a>, into the marshy lands of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mithila">Mithila</a>. The kingdom that grew there lay in what is now northern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bihar">Bihar</a> and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/terai">Terai</a> of <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/empire/kingdom-of-nepal">Nepal</a>, and it was one of the easternmost Vedic states.</p><h2>Rule and society</h2><p>Videha's court became a centre of speculative thought. In the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/brihadaranyaka-upanishad">Brihadaranyaka Upanishad</a> <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/janaka">King Janaka</a> hosts contests between <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/brahmins">Brahmin</a> teachers, and the sage <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/yajnavalkya">Yajnavalkya</a> expounds the doctrine of the self, atman, and its identity with brahman. Wealth came from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rice">rice</a> grown on the fertile floodplain. Later epic tradition made <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sita">Sita</a>, heroine of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ramayana">Ramayana</a>, the daughter of a Janaka of Videha.</p><h2>Decline and legacy</h2><p>By around 600 BCE the monarchy had given way to a republican order, and the Videhas became members of the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/vajji">Vajji</a> confederacy centred on <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vaishali">Vaishali</a>. <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/empire/kingdom-of-magadha">Magadha</a> conquered the confederacy in the 5th century BCE. The name <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mithila">Mithila</a> survives for the region, and its people still speak Maithili.</p></div><aside class="st-side"><figure class="st-fig"><img src="/images/states/videha.jpg" alt="Videha" width="320" height="245"><figcaption>Image: Avantiputra7 · CC BY-SA 3.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Late_Vedic_Culture_(1100-500_BCE).png" target="_blank" rel="noopener">source</a></figcaption></figure><dl class="st-facts"><dt>Years</dt><dd>1100 BCE – 600 BCE (approx.)</dd><dt>Centuries</dt><dd><a href="/centuries/12th-century-bce">12th century BCE</a> – <a href="/centuries/7th-century-bce">7th century BCE</a></dd><dt>Type</dt><dd>kingdom</dd><dt>Character</dt><dd>tribal</dd><dt>Capital</dt><dd>Mithila</dd><dt>Rulers</dt><dd>Janaka</dd><dt>Religion</dt><dd>Vedic religion</dd><dt>Language</dt><dd>Vedic Sanskrit</dd><dt>Today</dt><dd><a href="/countries/india">India</a>, <a href="/countries/nepal">Nepal</a></dd><dt>Also</dt><dd>Kingdom of Videha, Mithila (ancient)</dd></dl><figure class="st-locator"><a href="/atlas?year=-850&amp;focus=videha" aria-label="Videha on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="7005 1320 640 512" role="img" aria-label="Map: where Videha lay"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-tie" d="M7325 1576L7250 1479"/><path class="map-dot-n" d="M7250 1479h0"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M7325 1576h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M7325 1576h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks Mithila. <a href="/atlas?year=-850&amp;focus=videha">On the atlas &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></aside></div>
